faqs:
  - q: "What is the difference between encodeURIComponent and encodeURI?"
    a: "encodeURIComponent encodes all reserved punctuation characters (including &, =, ?, /, :, and #) into percent-encoded hex sequences. It is intended for encoding individual query parameter keys and values. In contrast, encodeURI preserves valid URL structure characters (;,/?:@&=+$#) and only encodes characters forbidden in standard URIs like spaces and Unicode glyphs."
  - q: "When should I use encodeURIComponent instead of encodeURI?"
    a: "Always use encodeURIComponent when preparing values to append to a query string (e.g., ?q=search+term or ?redirect_url=https://example.com). If you used encodeURI, symbols like & and = inside your value would be mistaken for additional query delimiters."
  - q: "How does this tool handle '+' plus signs in query strings?"
    a: "In application/x-www-form-urlencoded query strings, plus signs (+) commonly represent spaces. When decoding in Component mode, this tool translates both '+' and '%20' into standard space characters."
  - q: "Is my URL or private query parameter data sent to a server?"
    a: "No. All URL encoding, decoding, and query string table parsing run 100% locally in your web browser. Sensitive API tokens and query strings never leave your device."
  - q: "What is the maximum URL size supported?"
    a: "The tool supports inputs up to a 1 MB safety cap (1,048,576 characters). Live debounced processing ensures instant UI updates without lag."
sources:
